menu "Kern Configuration"

    choice KERN_BOARD
        prompt "Target board"
        default KERN_BOARD_WAVE_4B
        help
            Select the ESP32-P4 board variant.

        config KERN_BOARD_WAVE_4B
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WiFi6-Touch-LCD-4B (720x720)"

        config KERN_BOARD_WAVE_35
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WiFi6-Touch-LCD-3.5 (320x480)"

        config KERN_BOARD_WAVE_5
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WiFi6-Touch-LCD-5 (720x1280 MIPI DSI)"

        config KERN_BOARD_WAVE_43
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WiFi6-Touch-LCD-4.3 (480x800 MIPI DSI)"

        config KERN_BOARD_CROWPANEL
            bool "CrowPanel Advanced ESP32-P4 (7\" / 10.1\", 1024x600 MIPI DSI)"

        config KERN_BOARD_WAVE_7B
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WiFi6-Touch-LCD-7B (1024x600 MIPI DSI)"

        config KERN_BOARD_P4_PICO
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-Pico + Raspberry Pi 800x480 MIPI DSI display"
    endchoice

    choice KERN_PICO_DSI_BRIDGE
        prompt "ESP32-P4-Pico display bridge protocol"
        depends on KERN_BOARD_P4_PICO
        default KERN_PICO_DSI_BRIDGE_RPI_TS
        help
            Raspberry Pi DSI displays carry no LCD reset or backlight pins;
            a small MCU at I2C address 0x45 powers the panel and drives the
            backlight, and a DSI-to-RGB bridge sits in front of the glass.
            Two incompatible MCU/bridge families exist behind that address.

        config KERN_PICO_DSI_BRIDGE_RPI_TS
            bool "Raspberry Pi official 7\" touchscreen protocol (TC358762, 1 lane)"
            help
                The 2015 Raspberry Pi Touch Display and its many 800x480
                clones: Atmel MCU registers 0x80.., a Toshiba TC358762
                bridge configured over DSI generic writes, one data lane,
                FT5406 touch at 0x38.

        config KERN_PICO_DSI_BRIDGE_WAVESHARE
            bool "Waveshare DSI LCD protocol (registers 0xAA..0xC2, 2 lanes)"
            help
                Waveshare's Raspberry Pi DSI LCD family: MCU registers
                0xC0/0xC2/0xAC to power up, 0xAB/0xAA for backlight, 0xAD
                to enable, two data lanes, GT911 or FT5x06 touch.
    endchoice

    config KERN_PBKDF2_HW_SHA
        bool "Drive PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 on the P4 SHA accelerator"
        default y
        help
            Holds the SHA peripheral across batches of PBKDF2 iterations and
            precomputes the HMAC ipad/opad midstates, so an iteration costs two
            hardware block compressions instead of four compressions wrapped in
            four crypto-lock acquire/reset/release cycles.

            Output is byte-identical to the PSA path, which stays compiled in.
            Every call first runs a known-answer vector through the accelerated
            code and falls back to PSA if it disagrees.

            Turn this off to A/B against the PSA implementation on device.

    config KERN_PBKDF2_HW_SHA_CHUNK
        int "SHA hold length, in PBKDF2 iterations"
        depends on KERN_PBKDF2_HW_SHA
        default 1024
        range 16 65536
        help
            The peripheral is released and reacquired every this many
            iterations so AES and SHA stay available to the rest of the device.
            At the default one hold is about 6.6 ms -- under a 60 Hz frame --
            and reacquisition costs about 0.3% of the derivation.

            Lower it to shorten the outage, raise it to shave the reacquire
            cost.

    config KERN_PBKDF2_SELFTEST
        bool "Check and time both PBKDF2 paths at boot"
        default n
        help
            Runs known-answer vectors, then diffs the accelerated path against
            PSA across password, salt, iteration and output lengths, then times
            both. Results print prefixed PBKDF2.

            The host test suite cannot reach this code, so this is the only
            place the accelerated path is proven. Development only; it adds
            several seconds to boot.

endmenu
